 "result": "Call order (all in `src/tessel/` unless noted):\n\n1. `Tessel.run_full_request` (`app.py` ~L1012–1019): runs `preprocess_request` → `run_view` inside a `try`; any `Exception` goes to the next step.\n2. `Tessel.route_user_fault` (`app.py:865`): if the exception is an `HTTPException`, it delegates to `route_http_fault`. Otherwise it picks a handler, and if none matches it re-raises.\n3. `Tessel.route_http_fault` (`app.py:830`): handles HTTP exceptions, using the picked handler if one exists.\n4. `Tessel._pick_fault_handler` (`sansio/app.py:869`): both of the above call this to choose the handler.\n   - Lookup is in `error_handler_spec[name][code]`, which is populated by `register_error_handler` (`sansio/scaffold.py:650`).\n   - Precedence, most specific first:\n     1. bundle handler for the status code\n     2. app handler for the status code\n     3. bundle handler for the exception class\n     4. app handler for the exception class\n   - Within each map, it walks the exception's MRO, so a subclass handler wins over a parent's.\n5. `Tessel.route_unhandled_fault` (`app.py:897`): fallback from `wsgi_app` (`app.py:1600`) if the exception escapes `run_full_request`, or if `route_user_fault` re-raises. It wraps the error in an `InternalServerError` and calls `_pick_fault_handler` again (L943), which finds a 500 handler or falls back to the default response.",
